# Recent advances on ISRU technologies and study of microgravity impact on blood cells for deep space exploration## Abstract This work analyzes advancements in ISRU technology for deep space colonization. It discusses methods for producing tangible goods for industrial or civil installations on the Moon, Mars, or asteroids using in situ regolith. Key developments include a process based on self-propagating high-temperature synthesis (SHS) for ceramic composites and the use of sintered regolith for thermal energy storage.## 1. Introduction In-Situ Resource Utilization (ISRU) and In Situ Fabrication and Repair (ISFR) are essential for colonization. The Italian task force (University of Cagliari, INAF, CNR, etc.) has focused on: 1) SHS reactions for structural elements; 2) ISRU for consumables (food, fuel, oxygen); 3) Optical characterization of regolith for solar absorbers; 4) Microalgae cultivation; 5) Microgravity impacts on human cells.## 2. Manufacturing physical assets in extraterrestrial environments Cao et al. (2011) developed an ISFR process involving regolith extraction, enrichment (e.g., iron oxide), and mixing with aluminum powder. The heart of the process is the SHS reaction, occurring at 1,800-2,000 K. These reactions propagate spontaneously upon local ignition. Compressive strength values of the obtained SHS samples range from 25.8–27.2 MPa, significantly higher than geothermite-based processes (10–18 MPa). Experiments were also performed under microgravity via parabolic flights (Airbus-300).## 4. ISRU process to sustain human missions on Mars Integration of ISRU with ECLSS aims for crew self-sufficiency. A design case for a crew of six requires production of oxygen, water, and propellants. Table 1 summarizes employed resources (Mars atmosphere: 122.3 kg/h; Regolith: 5,393 kg/h) and outputs (Oxygen: 41.66 kg/h; Edible biomass: 0.09 kg/h).## 6. Optical characterization of lunar regolith simulant Sintered samples of JSC-1A lunar regolith were prepared using Spark Plasma Sintering (SPS). Sintering for 3 min at 700°C and 900°C reached relative densities of 86% and 98% respectively. Sintered blocks showed superior solar absorptance compared to powder form, making them suitable for solar receivers and thermal energy storage (TES).## 8. Concluding remarks Novel SHS techniques can produce structural elements on Mars/Moon. Integrated ISRU-ECLSS systems can sustain crews. Sintered regolith properties facilitate thermal energy harvesting. Microgravity effects on red blood cells (RBC) include changes in stiffness and cytoskeletal architecture.
